---
// Lesson 12.1b — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 1
// rpg jam: build the board — 2D array/list-of-lists, cells, one move.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>12.1b — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 1</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Core-loop day again (<a href="10.1b.html">10.1b</a> rules, grid
    edition): by the end of class, a board exists in DATA, one move
    works end to end, and the win/lose stub prints. The one new
    idea today is the data structure that runs half the games ever
    made: a 2D array.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the board as data (list of lists):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>const int W = 8; const int H = 8;
private int[,] grid = new int[W, H];       // 0 = empty, 1 = player, 2 = wall...

// or the AP way — a list OF lists:
// grid[ROW][COL] — the exam writes aList[r][c] exactly like this</code></pre>
    <p>Print the whole grid to the console each frame for now —
    Debug.Log row by row. You must be able to READ your board in
    text before you can trust it in sprites.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — one move.</strong> The player cursor at
    (x, y): W/S/A/D changes it by ±1 — but ONLY inside the array's
    bounds. The two bugs of every grid game, met now:</p>
    <pre><code>int nx = x + dx;
int ny = y + dy;
if (nx &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; nx &lt; W &amp;&amp; ny &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ny &lt; H)  // BOUNDS — always
{
    x = nx;
    y = ny;
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the symmetry hook.</strong> Whatever your
    pitch promised, wire the CHEAPEST version of it today: mirror
    the cursor on the other half (<code>x2 = W - 1 - x</code> — one
    line!), or spawn the enemy AI at the mirrored start. That one
    line IS the design; everything else elaborates it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — lose/win stub.</strong> Touch a hazard cell
    → print "lose"; reach the far cell → print "win". The loop
    exists even if nothing is drawn.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Grid classics</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You mixed x/y and row/col. Why is grid[y, x] the
                CONVENTION (row first) — and what does a swap
                look like on screen (a square board LIES — it
                looks correct!)? Test on a RECTANGLE.</li>
            <li>Off-by-one at the edge: W or W-1 for the last
                column? (<a href="11.4a.html">11.4a</a>'s
                wrong-answer tell, costing you a day — count
                from 0.)</li>
            <li>The mirrored cursor walks off the opposite edge
                (you bounded one side, not the mirror). Which
                LINE bounds the mirror — and why did the
                cheap-symmetry trick not save you HERE?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Loop proof</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Console-drawn board + one working move + win/lose
                print. Commit + push: <code>"12.1b: grid core"</code>.</li>
            <li>Sticky note: tomorrow's riskiest thing
                (<a href="10.1c.html">10.1c</a> ritual — it
                works; keep it).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build and PRINT a grid; explain grid[y, x] row-first</li>
            <li>Move a cursor with bounds checks on both sides</li>
            <li>Wire the cheapest version of your symmetry in one line</li>
            <li>End the day with the loop running in text</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html">Lists &amp; Arrays</a> — 2D and bounds patterns</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-12/index.html">Unit 12: Season 2</a> — jam days map</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="12.1c.html">12.1c — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 2</a></p>
</div>`;
